1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:04,139
The digital SAT changes
based on how you do.

2
00:00:04,159 --> 00:00:07,780
That one fact has turned
into a lot of rumors.

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:09,660
Every question adapts.

4
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:13,180
The easier second module
has an official score cap.

5
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:16,770
Hard questions are worth
some fixed number of points.

6
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:20,570
Those rumors cause real
mistakes on test day.

7
00:00:20,679 --> 00:00:23,580
Students rush module one to save time,

8
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:27,939
panic when module two feels
hard, or leave questions blank.

9
00:00:27,959 --> 00:00:29,969
All three can cost points.

10
00:00:30,419 --> 00:00:34,028
In this video: how the
test actually adapts,

11
00:00:34,099 --> 00:00:37,039
using College Board's own explanations,

12
00:00:37,059 --> 00:00:42,629
what that means for how you take it,
and which of those rumors hold up.

13
00:00:43,027 --> 00:00:44,966
Start with the shape of the test.

14
00:00:44,986 --> 00:00:46,917
There are two sections.

15
00:00:46,987 --> 00:00:50,007
Reading and Writing
comes first, then Math,

16
00:00:50,027 --> 00:00:52,317
with a ten-minute break in between.

17
00:00:52,586 --> 00:00:55,927
Each section is split into
two modules of equal length,

18
00:00:55,947 --> 00:00:58,756
and each module is timed separately.

19
00:00:59,183 --> 00:01:01,843
Reading and Writing modules
are thirty-two minutes,

20
00:01:01,863 --> 00:01:04,123
with twenty-seven questions each.

21
00:01:04,143 --> 00:01:06,443
Math modules are thirty-five minutes,

22
00:01:06,463 --> 00:01:08,562
with twenty-two questions each.

23
00:01:08,582 --> 00:01:12,923
That's two hours and fourteen
minutes of testing in total.

24
00:01:12,943 --> 00:01:17,483
Most questions are multiple choice, and
some math questions ask you to type in

25
00:01:17,503 --> 00:01:18,513
your answer.

26
00:01:19,011 --> 00:01:23,981
College Board calls this
multistage adaptive testing.

27
00:01:24,051 --> 00:01:28,511
The test adapts in stages,
one module at a time.

28
00:01:28,531 --> 00:01:31,390
It doesn't change after
every single question.

29
00:01:31,410 --> 00:01:36,391
You answer a set of questions, and the
next set depends on how you did on that

30
00:01:36,411 --> 00:01:37,411
set.

31
00:01:37,492 --> 00:01:41,632
Module one has a broad
mix of easy, medium,

32
00:01:41,652 --> 00:01:42,792
and hard questions.

33
00:01:42,812 --> 00:01:45,302
Everyone gets that same kind of mix.

34
00:01:45,332 --> 00:01:49,111
Then, based on how you do on module one,

35
00:01:49,131 --> 00:01:53,182
module two is either more
difficult or less difficult.

36
00:01:53,692 --> 00:01:55,862
Here's what that means for how it feels.

37
00:01:56,212 --> 00:01:59,312
Module two is aimed at your level.

38
00:01:59,332 --> 00:02:03,542
So whichever one you get, it's
normal for it to feel challenging.

39
00:02:03,771 --> 00:02:07,032
A tough module two is the design working.

40
00:02:07,052 --> 00:02:09,501
It isn't a sign that you're failing.

41
00:02:09,945 --> 00:02:13,164
In College Board's words, you're
routed to a second module,

42
00:02:13,184 --> 00:02:16,365
which has a different
mix of difficulty levels.

43
00:02:16,385 --> 00:02:19,164
And this happens inside each section.

44
00:02:19,184 --> 00:02:24,235
Your math module two is based on
how you did in math module one.

45
00:02:24,745 --> 00:02:26,315
Why build it this way?

46
00:02:26,585 --> 00:02:31,445
College Board says it makes the test
shorter while keeping it as precise and

47
00:02:31,465 --> 00:02:37,124
reliable as a longer test, because module
two is matched to your level instead

48
00:02:37,144 --> 00:02:39,314
of giving everyone the same questions.

49
00:02:39,753 --> 00:02:43,052
Inside a module, you have freedom.

50
00:02:43,072 --> 00:02:46,413
You can move back and forth,
preview questions coming up,

51
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:48,693
and mark questions to come back to.

52
00:02:48,713 --> 00:02:52,123
But once you leave a module,
you can't return to it.

53
00:02:52,596 --> 00:02:54,726
Now scoring.

54
00:02:54,756 --> 00:02:59,696
Your section score is based on
all the questions in both modules,

55
00:02:59,716 --> 00:03:01,566
not just module two.

56
00:03:01,755 --> 00:03:06,165
College Board uses a method
called item response theory.

57
00:03:06,396 --> 00:03:10,176
Your score depends on which
questions you got right and wrong,

58
00:03:10,196 --> 00:03:13,766
including how difficult they
were, not just how many.

59
00:03:14,191 --> 00:03:18,010
That's why College Board says two
students with the same number of correct

60
00:03:18,030 --> 00:03:21,481
answers can end up with
different section scores.

61
00:03:21,934 --> 00:03:26,114
The same College Board page says the
method also considers whether your pattern

62
00:03:26,134 --> 00:03:28,744
of answers looks like guessing.

63
00:03:28,854 --> 00:03:30,784
Does that mean guessing hurts?

64
00:03:31,134 --> 00:03:31,954
No.

65
00:03:31,974 --> 00:03:36,874
That page also says that for most students
trying their best on every question,

66
00:03:36,894 --> 00:03:39,924
it's better to guess than
leave a question blank.

67
00:03:39,944 --> 00:03:44,284
So try every question for real,
and when you're out of ideas,

68
00:03:44,304 --> 00:03:45,304
guess.

69
00:03:45,651 --> 00:03:47,751
Here's the part that should calm you down.

70
00:03:47,771 --> 00:03:52,431
College Board says a range of section
scores is possible no matter which second

71
00:03:52,451 --> 00:03:53,391
module you see.

72
00:03:53,411 --> 00:03:56,511
You aren't helped just
for seeing a harder set,

73
00:03:56,531 --> 00:03:59,060
or hurt just for seeing an easier one.

74
00:03:59,525 --> 00:04:00,425
Two more facts.

75
00:04:00,445 --> 00:04:04,985
Each module includes two pretest questions
that don't count toward your score,

76
00:04:05,005 --> 00:04:07,105
and you can't tell which ones they are.

77
00:04:07,125 --> 00:04:12,144
And scores use the familiar scale:
four hundred to sixteen hundred total,

78
00:04:12,164 --> 00:04:14,854
two hundred to eight hundred per section.

79
00:04:15,291 --> 00:04:16,911
And notice what's missing.

80
00:04:16,931 --> 00:04:22,071
College Board doesn't publish a
points-per-question chart or a scoring formula.

81
00:04:22,091 --> 00:04:27,471
So if someone online says a hard
question is worth exactly some number of

82
00:04:27,491 --> 00:04:30,501
points, that's a guess,
not an official number.

83
00:04:30,943 --> 00:04:32,673
So what do you do with all this?

84
00:04:32,823 --> 00:04:34,923
First, module one matters.

85
00:04:34,943 --> 00:04:39,352
It counts toward your score, and it
decides which module two you get.

86
00:04:39,463 --> 00:04:42,512
Treat the questions you
know how to do as must-gets.

87
00:04:43,097 --> 00:04:45,117
That doesn't mean rushing.

88
00:04:45,137 --> 00:04:47,037
It means the opposite.

89
00:04:47,057 --> 00:04:49,677
A careless miss in module
one can cost you twice:

90
00:04:49,697 --> 00:04:54,556
once as a wrong answer, and again in
which module two you're routed to.

91
00:04:54,576 --> 00:05:00,267
So read carefully, and reread what each
question asks for before you answer.

92
00:05:00,718 --> 00:05:03,057
And remember, module one is a broad mix,

93
00:05:03,077 --> 00:05:05,738
so it has hard questions in it too.

94
00:05:05,758 --> 00:05:09,138
Missing a hard one doesn't
mean module one went badly.

95
00:05:09,158 --> 00:05:11,447
Don't let it shake you
on the next question.

96
00:05:11,937 --> 00:05:16,237
Second, don't let one hard
question eat module one.

97
00:05:16,257 --> 00:05:19,876
You can move around inside
a module, so mark it,

98
00:05:19,896 --> 00:05:23,147
make your best guess, and
come back if there's time.

99
00:05:23,377 --> 00:05:28,027
Five minutes on one question is five
minutes taken from several others.

100
00:05:28,451 --> 00:05:32,701
Third, if module two
feels hard, don't panic.

101
00:05:32,971 --> 00:05:37,901
A harder module two may simply
mean module one went well.

102
00:05:37,971 --> 00:05:40,741
If it feels easy, don't relax either.

103
00:05:40,850 --> 00:05:43,551
Trying to figure out which
module you got doesn't help.

104
00:05:43,571 --> 00:05:45,421
Just answer the next question.

105
00:05:45,850 --> 00:05:47,550
Picture two students.

106
00:05:47,570 --> 00:05:51,510
One does well on module one,
gets the harder module two,

107
00:05:51,530 --> 00:05:56,140
and walks out thinking the test went
badly because the end felt brutal.

108
00:05:56,290 --> 00:06:00,499
The other gets the easier module
two and walks out feeling great.

109
00:06:00,690 --> 00:06:04,860
How module two felt doesn't
tell either of them their score.

110
00:06:05,350 --> 00:06:07,440
Same goes for a strange question.

111
00:06:07,470 --> 00:06:11,610
Two questions in every module
are unscored pretest questions,

112
00:06:11,630 --> 00:06:14,210
and they look exactly like the rest.

113
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:16,690
If one feels weird, it
might not even count.

114
00:06:16,710 --> 00:06:20,850
You can't know, so give it your best shot
and move on without letting it rattle

115
00:06:20,870 --> 00:06:21,865
you.

116
00:06:21,885 --> 00:06:24,425
Fourth, never leave a blank.

117
00:06:24,445 --> 00:06:27,465
College Board says there's
no penalty for guessing,

118
00:06:27,485 --> 00:06:30,504
and that it's better to guess
than leave a question blank,

119
00:06:30,524 --> 00:06:34,065
especially if you can eliminate
one or two choices first.

120
00:06:34,085 --> 00:06:39,255
Before a module ends, open the question
menu and fill every empty square.

121
00:06:39,750 --> 00:06:40,410
Quick check.

122
00:06:40,430 --> 00:06:41,650
Four statements.

123
00:06:41,670 --> 00:06:44,440
Decide which are myths
and which are facts.

124
00:06:44,550 --> 00:06:48,159
One, the test adapts after every question.

125
00:06:48,270 --> 00:06:51,889
Two, your section score
counts both modules.

126
00:06:51,909 --> 00:06:55,129
Three, if you get the easier module two,

127
00:06:55,149 --> 00:06:58,570
there's an official maximum
score you can't go above.

128
00:06:58,590 --> 00:07:01,360
Four, there's no penalty for guessing.

129
00:07:01,836 --> 00:07:03,046
Pause and decide.

130
00:07:09,936 --> 00:07:11,316
Number one is a myth.

131
00:07:11,336 --> 00:07:15,956
The SAT adapts by module,
not question by question.

132
00:07:15,976 --> 00:07:19,356
That's why you can preview
upcoming questions in a module:

133
00:07:19,376 --> 00:07:21,356
the set is already in place.

134
00:07:21,376 --> 00:07:24,906
An easy-feeling question doesn't
mean you missed the last one.

135
00:07:25,436 --> 00:07:27,766
Number two is a fact.

136
00:07:28,235 --> 00:07:33,815
Your section score is based on
all the questions in both modules.

137
00:07:33,835 --> 00:07:36,765
Module one isn't just a placement quiz.

138
00:07:37,290 --> 00:07:39,589
Number three is a myth, as stated.

139
00:07:39,609 --> 00:07:44,870
College Board doesn't publish a
maximum score for either second module.

140
00:07:44,890 --> 00:07:49,030
So any cap number you see
online is someone's estimate,

141
00:07:49,050 --> 00:07:50,779
not an official number.

142
00:07:51,251 --> 00:07:55,951
What College Board does say is that a
range of section scores is possible no

143
00:07:55,971 --> 00:07:58,301
matter which second module you see.

144
00:07:58,331 --> 00:08:00,951
It doesn't say where the
top of that range is.

145
00:08:00,971 --> 00:08:05,550
Either way, your job doesn't
change: be careful in module one,

146
00:08:05,570 --> 00:08:07,621
and keep going in module two.

147
00:08:08,037 --> 00:08:10,407
Number four is a fact.

148
00:08:10,836 --> 00:08:14,807
College Board says there's
no penalty for guessing.

149
00:08:14,877 --> 00:08:17,007
A blank can't be right.

150
00:08:17,036 --> 00:08:18,526
A guess might be.

151
00:08:18,964 --> 00:08:23,664
Put it together, and the adaptive
SAT comes down to a simple plan.

152
00:08:23,684 --> 00:08:28,304
Module one: careful, especially on
the questions you know how to do.

153
00:08:28,324 --> 00:08:31,704
Module two: calm, no matter how it feels.

154
00:08:31,724 --> 00:08:36,214
And in every module: mark
and move, and no blanks.

155
00:08:36,672 --> 00:08:37,612
One more thing.

156
00:08:37,632 --> 00:08:43,932
Each module has its own clock, so rushing
module one doesn't buy you time later.

157
00:08:43,952 --> 00:08:47,452
Use the time you have, because
once you leave a module,

158
00:08:47,472 --> 00:08:49,001
you can't come back.

159
00:08:49,522 --> 00:08:53,942
The best way to get used to it is a
full-length practice test in Bluebook.

160
00:08:53,962 --> 00:08:58,382
College Board says those use the
same multistage adaptive model,

161
00:08:58,402 --> 00:09:00,862
and they're timed like the real test.

162
00:09:00,882 --> 00:09:05,622
Afterward, your scores and answer
explanations show up in My Practice,

163
00:09:05,642 --> 00:09:08,892
so you can see where
module one went wrong.

164
00:09:09,387 --> 00:09:10,247
Recap.

165
00:09:10,267 --> 00:09:14,317
The SAT adapts by module, not by question.

166
00:09:14,507 --> 00:09:18,887
Module one is a broad mix,
and it decides module two.

167
00:09:18,907 --> 00:09:20,207
Both modules count.

168
00:09:20,227 --> 00:09:23,276
There's no official
points-per-question chart.

169
00:09:23,386 --> 00:09:25,717
And there's no penalty for guessing.

170
00:09:26,213 --> 00:09:31,793
Next in this series: SAT pacing, and how
to finish both modules without rushing

171
00:09:31,813 --> 00:09:32,713
the end.

172
00:09:32,733 --> 00:09:35,223
If this cleared things up, subscribe.
